  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how a dysfunctional mitochondrion may contribute to problems across several organs, emphasizing a systemic role in heart failure. A large mitochondrion on the left is linked by dashed lines to the heart, skeletal muscle, and kidney, suggesting that mitochondrial dysfunction affects each of these tissues.\n\nThe heart is presented as the central clinical consequence, with worsening heart failure accompanied by declining left ventricular systolic performance and contractility, as well as impaired diastolic relaxation. This indicates both pumping and filling abnormalities. Skeletal muscle involvement is shown as exercise intolerance, weakness or fatigue, and insulin resistance, highlighting how reduced cellular energy handling can extend beyond the heart and limit physical capacity. The kidney is associated with renal dysfunction and poor blood pressure control, implying that mitochondrial impairment may also contribute to cardiorenal complications.\n\nOverall, the graphic conveys that mitochondrial dysfunction is not just a localized cellular issue but a shared mechanism linked to progressive multisystem impairment in heart failure, connecting reduced energy metabolism to cardiac decline, muscle symptoms, and kidney-related complications.",
